       <p>If a metro had no ACS row, the page would fall back to 50% single for women (6 points higher for men, 10 for nonbinary people) and to the metro's adult bachelor's rate for degrees, times 1.25 for women, 0.97 for men and 1.15 for nonbinary people.</p>
       <p>The map puts each metro at its internal point from the Census Bureau's <a href="https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/time-series/geo/gazetteer-files.html">2023 Gazetteer</a>, with state outlines from <a href="https://github.com/topojson/us-atlas">us-atlas</a>, in the Albers USA projection that moves Alaska and Hawaii into insets. Circle area is proportional to the number of possibilities on the apps right now.</p>
-      <p>The range under the main number comes from a Monte Carlo simulation. The page redraws every input 2,000 times from a distribution that reflects how sure we are of it, runs the funnel for each draw, and shows the middle 80% of the results (10th to 90th percentile). The spreads come from the sources themselves: the Census Bureau's published margins of error for each metro's single people by age band and degree share (standard error = margin of error ÷ 1.645, summed across bands as root sum of squares); survey sample sizes for Gallup's openness rates and PRRI's faith shares; ±8% for Pew's share of singles who are looking and ±3.5% for the cohabiting cut; ±7 points on the progressive offset, ±5 points on the secular-leaning base and ±15% on the overlap boost, since those are modeling choices; ±25% each on the two attraction rates, which spans the published yes-rates from 22% to 49%; ±6% on the kids step; and ±5% to ±30% on the diet rates, depending on the survey. The page also reports which group of inputs drives most of the range, from how much each varies the result on its own. The range only covers uncertainty in the inputs. It does not cover errors in how the funnel is built, like assuming the steps are independent.</p>
+      <p>The range under the main number comes from a Monte Carlo simulation. The page redraws every input 2,000 times from a distribution that reflects how sure we are of it, runs the funnel for each draw, and shows the middle 80% of the results (10th to 90th percentile). The spreads come from the sources themselves: the Census Bureau's published margins of error for each metro's single people by age band and degree share (standard error = margin of error ÷ 1.645, summed across bands as root sum of squares); survey sample sizes for Gallup's openness rates and PRRI's faith shares; ±8% for Pew's share of singles who are looking and ±3.5% for the cohabiting cut; ±7 points on the progressive offset and ±5 points on the secular-leaning base, since those are modeling choices; ±15% on the odds ratio between politics and faith (±24% among degree holders) and ±10% on how a degree shifts politics and faith, from bootstrap resamples of the survey; ±25% each on the two attraction rates, which spans the published yes-rates from 22% to 49%; ±6% on the kids step; and ±5% to ±30% on the diet rates, depending on the survey. The page also reports which group of inputs drives most of the range, from how much each varies the result on its own. The range only covers uncertainty in the inputs. It does not cover errors in how the funnel is built, like assuming the steps are independent.</p>
       <p>Not every single person is available or looking. In Pew's 2022 survey, 20% of people who were neither married nor living with a partner were in a committed relationship (<a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2023/01/PI_2023.02.02_Onilne-Dating_FINAL.pdf">topline, p. 64</a>), and Pew doesn't break that out by age, so the page removes 20% at every age. Of the rest, Pew's 2019 survey found 67% of men and 61% of women under 40 looking for a relationship or dates, against 55% of men and only 29% of women 40 and older (<a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2020/08/PSDT_08.20.20.dating-relationships.full_.report.pdf">report, p. 18</a>). By 2022 the share looking had fallen from 61% to 50% for single men and from 38% to 35% for single women (<a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2023/02/08/for-valentines-day-5-facts-about-single-americans/">Pew</a>), so the page scales each age split by that drop: 55% and 45% for men, 56% and 27% for women. Nonbinary people get the average. The same step applies to the competition, since people who aren't looking aren't competing.</p>
       <p>The competition ratio divides the single, looking, degree-holding people of the genders you picked by every single, looking, degree-holding person in the same age range who is open to dating them, whatever their gender. A straight man in a metro with equal numbers of men and women gets about 0.93, because some women also date women. The sex ratio in the list is simpler: single, looking, degree-holding women per man. Because far fewer women than men over 40 are looking, it tilts toward men at older ages. Unchecking the degree filter drops the degree step from your pool and the competition alike.</p>
       <p>The values estimate is a model, not survey data. It starts from the 2024 vote, with county results summed for each metro (<a href="https://github.com/tonmcg/US_County_Level_Election_Results_08-24">tonmcg county results</a>). Connecticut and Alaska don't report by county in that file, so their metros use the statewide Harris share. The progressive share is the Harris share plus 20 points, capped at 90%. The secular-leaning share combines two religion sources: <code>40% + (unaffiliated share − 23.1%) + 0.3 × (48.6% − congregation share)</code>. The unaffiliated share is the metro's share of adults with no religion, from PRRI's county estimates in its <a href="https://www.prri.org/census/">Census of American Religion</a>, weighted by population across each metro's counties. The congregation share is the share of residents counted as members of a congregation in the <a href="https://www.usreligioncensus.org/node/1639">2020 U.S. Religion Census</a>; metros created since 2020 use the old metro or county they came from. Both figures are metro averages across the atlas (23.1% and 48.6%). The PRRI estimates carry most of the weight because they measure what people say they believe. The congregation share adds how much organized religion is around, but only lightly, since Catholic parishes count everyone they baptized. PRRI's county model already uses education and the vote, so neither enters this formula again. Men are shifted 10 points less progressive and 4 points more secular, and nonbinary people 25 points more progressive and 15 points more secular.</p>
-      <p>Politics and faith can be changed in "Who you're looking for". Conservative is everyone outside the progressive share above, so the two always add to 100%. The faith shares come from the same <a href="https://www.prri.org/census/">PRRI county estimates</a>, weighted by population across each metro's counties: Christian (Catholic, Protestant, Latter-day Saint, Orthodox and Jehovah's Witness), Catholic, Protestant, Latter-day Saint, Jewish, Muslim, Hindu and Buddhist. PRRI has no Pagan or Wiccan estimate, so the page starts from 0.3% of adults (<a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/religious-landscape-study/">Pew's 2014 Religious Landscape Study</a>) and scales it by each metro's PRRI share of other religions against the atlas average of 1.44%. These are shares of all adults, not of young singles, so they will be off for groups that skew young or old. The 1.5 overlap boost applies only to matching pairs, progressive with secular-leaning and conservative with a Christian faith, because those go together more often than chance.</p>
+      <p>Politics and faith can be changed in "Who you're looking for". Conservative is everyone outside the progressive share above, so the two always add to 100%. The faith shares come from the same <a href="https://www.prri.org/census/">PRRI county estimates</a>, weighted by population across each metro's counties: Christian (Catholic, Protestant, Latter-day Saint, Orthodox and Jehovah's Witness), Catholic, Protestant, Latter-day Saint, Jewish, Muslim, Hindu and Buddhist. PRRI has no Pagan or Wiccan estimate, so the page starts from 0.3% of adults (<a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/religious-landscape-study/">Pew's 2014 Religious Landscape Study</a>) and scales it by each metro's PRRI share of other religions against the atlas average of 1.44%. These are shares of all adults, not of young singles, so they will be off for groups that skew young or old. Matching pairs, progressive with secular-leaning and conservative with a Christian faith, go together more often than chance. The share who fit both comes from the odds ratio between politics and faith among single 25 to 35 year olds in the <a href="https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/X11EP6">2024 Cooperative Election Study</a>: 1.8 for women and 2.3 for men for progressive with no religion, and 2.0 and 1.9 for conservative with a Christian faith. Worked out this way (a Plackett joint), it can never exceed either share on its own. Degree holders are more progressive (odds 1.4 to 1.4 times higher), more often Christian (1.2 to 1.3 times) and less often unaffiliated, and their politics and faith line up more tightly (odds ratios of 4.1 to 5.0), so the degree filter uses those figures. Progressive counts Democrats and leaners, with independents as half.</p>
       <p>The diet step uses national rates. In <a href="https://news.gallup.com/poll/510038/identify-vegetarian-vegan.aspx">Gallup's 2023 poll</a>, 4% of Americans were vegetarian (6% of women, 2% of men) and 1% vegan, and 9% of liberals were vegetarian against 3% of moderates and conservatives, so vegetarian and vegan rates are scaled by 2.25 for progressives and 0.75 for conservatives. Vegan rates split by gender the same way as vegetarian. An <a href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/nutrition/articles/10.3389/fnut.2023.1113792/full">analysis of NHANES</a> found 4.7% of adults eat a pescatarian diet, 59.5% of them women; the pescatarian option also counts vegetarians, and only that vegetarian part is scaled by politics. Kosher and halal follow the local faith shares: 17% of Jewish Americans keep kosher at home (<a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2021/05/11/jewish-practices-and-customs/">Pew 2020</a>), and 83% of Muslim Americans only buy halal or prefer it (<a href="https://ispu.org/halal-preferences/">ISPU 2022</a>).</p>
       <p>The adult bachelor's rate in the fallback above is the county share of adults 25 and older with a bachelor's degree or higher, from the 2015 to 2019 ACS (<a href="https://github.com/OpenIntroStat/usdata">OpenIntro usdata</a>), weighted by population across each metro's counties and scaled up to today's national rate (32.1% to 36.9%). The degree step in the funnel uses Table B15001.</p>
